(1706454, 225221, 17507, '<td valign="top">This picture is amazing. Both my girlfriend and I were stunned. I''d have placed this first if I could! Congratulations on creating a beautiful photograph.</td>', 'This picture is amazing. Both my girlfriend and I were stunned. I''d have placed this first if I could! Congratulations on creating a beautiful photograph.', '2005-09-14 01:28:31.000000', NULL, 0),
(1706482, 227952, 32147, '<td valign="top">Thanks everyone for the comments!! This is the first image that I have done that has received so many remarks.  <br/> <br/>For those of you who asked, this is a simple zoom blur taken of a tree with the sun directly behind the trunk. <br/> <br/>A zoom blur is easily done with a good tripod and any zoom lens - simply lock the camera down tight, choose a good aperture and a fairly slow shutter - in this case 1/25th of a second.  While you release the shutter with one hand, your free hand takes hold of the zoom ring on the lens and rotates in or out while the shutter is open.  The length of the lens and the amount of twist you give the zoom ring will determine how long the blurred lines are.  Pretty simple, eh?  Its a cool effect and since it is all done in camera, it tends to add a unique approach to some shots.<br/> <br/>Thanks again for all of the comments - I do appreciate everyone''s feedback!<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2005-09-14 00:56:04.</i></td>', 'Thanks everyone for the comments!! This is the first image that I have done that has received so many remarks.   For those of you who asked, this is a simple zoom blur taken of a tree with the sun directly behind the trunk.  A zoom blur is easily done with a good tripod and any zoom lens - simply lock the camera down tight, choose a good aperture and a fairly slow shutter - in this case 1/25th of a second.  While you release the shutter with one hand, your free hand takes hold of the zoom ring on the lens and rotates in or out while the shutter is open.  The length of the lens and the amount of twist you give the zoom ring will determine how long the blurred lines are.  Pretty simple, eh?  Its a cool effect and since it is all done in camera, it tends to add a unique approach to some shots. Thanks again for all of the comments - I do appreciate everyone''s feedback!', '2005-09-14 01:33:29.000000', '2005-09-14 01:56:04.000000', 0),
(1706544, 225532, 27164, '<td valign="top">Excellent use of monochromatic color.  A stunning image.</td>', 'Excellent use of monochromatic color.  A stunning image.', '2005-09-14 01:44:55.000000', NULL, 0),
(1706546, 227493, 43410, '<td valign="top">thanks so much everyone for the great comments and the voting.  this was my first submission here.  i''ve been watching the site for some time now, and finally joined, and i love it.  i can''t wait to grow as a photographer here w/ you all.<br/> <br/>take care, and thanks again.<br/> <br/>-jon rowe</td>', 'thanks so much everyone for the great comments and the voting.  this was my first submission here.  i''ve been watching the site for some time now, and finally joined, and i love it.  i can''t wait to grow as a photographer here w/ you all. take care, and thanks again. -jon rowe', '2005-09-14 01:45:03.000000', NULL, 0),
